# Copy this file to `.env` before starting anything:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `pnpm dev`, `pnpm dev:remote`, and `docker compose` run from this directory all read
# this file, and it is the single source of truth for the PocketBase operator
# account. Both refuse to start without it (the container also refuses to render
# without DBRAINX_ADMIN_EMAIL). `.env` is gitignored — never commit real values.

# --- PocketBase operator account (required) --------------------------------
# The only credential that can open the PocketBase Dashboard. The application
# itself never uses it: user identity is the `users` collection, and the API
# reaches PocketBase with each request's end-user token. It is required anyway,
# so a machine never ends up with PocketBase's unusable placeholder account.
DBRAINX_ADMIN_EMAIL=
DBRAINX_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Preferred over DBRAINX_ADMIN_PASSWORD for a deployment with mounted secrets: a
# file holding only the password, which keeps it out of the shell history and out
# of `docker inspect`. Both dev commands resolve a relative path against this repository
# root; inside a container it must be a path the container can read
# (e.g. a mounted /run/secrets/... file), and its trailing newline is trimmed.
#   printf %s "$(openssl rand -base64 24)" > .pb-admin-password
# DBRAINX_ADMIN_PASSWORD_FILE=.pb-admin-password

# --- Development ports (pnpm dev / pnpm dev:remote) -----------------------
# Both root commands read this section, so one place moves the whole stack.
# A variable set in the shell wins over this file.
DBRAINX_WEB_PORT=3000
DBRAINX_SERVER_PORT=3001
DBRAINX_PB_HTTP=127.0.0.1:8090
# Remote development gateway. The browser uses https://p<port>.<dev domain>,
# and the proxy rule p<port>.<dev domain> -> 127.0.0.1:<port> matches it.
DBRAINX_GATEWAY_PORT=8080
# Printed at startup only; nothing listens on it. Example: dev.example.com
DBRAINX_DEV_DOMAIN=

# --- Docker Compose only (pnpm dev / pnpm dev:remote ignore these) ---------# Interface and port the gateway publishes; keep it on loopback and put a TLS
# proxy in front for anything reachable from outside.
DBRAINX_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
DBRAINX_PORT=3000
DBRAINX_AUTO_UPDATE=false
# Leave empty unless your proxy rewrites the `Host` header (an extra CDN hop, or
# `proxy_set_header Host $proxy_host`). The bundled nginx forwards the original
# `Host`, so the default is already correct — and it must stay empty locally,
# where it would be inert anyway. When set it must equal the browser's exact
# `Origin` (scheme, host, non-default port; browsers omit `:443`), because it
# replaces rather than extends the usual rule. An empty value behaves as unset.
DBRAINX_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=

# --- Chunked uploads -------------------------------------------------------
# A file larger than 32 MiB is uploaded in 8 MiB chunks; the API spools them
# here, assembles one file, hands that to PocketBase and deletes the spool.
# The directory therefore needs room for one upload in flight (up to 2 GiB),
# and it must be on a filesystem with space for the assembled copy as well.
# Defaults to `<system temp>/dbrainx-uploads`; abandoned sessions are swept
# after 24 hours.
# DBRAINX_UPLOAD_DIR=

# --- Media import (video and audio) ---------------------------------------
# The API downloads a video or an audio track from a pasted media-site address
# with yt-dlp and stores it as a resource. `pnpm dev` downloads a current yt-dlp
# release into `.cache/yt-dlp/` on first use; the server container image installs
# one. Leave the two yt-dlp values empty to use those binaries. Set `YT_DLP_PATH`
# to an absolute path or another binary name to use a different install. Set
# `YT_DLP_DOWNLOAD_BASE` to a mirror of the release download directory when
# GitHub releases are not reachable.
# YT_DLP_PATH=
# YT_DLP_DOWNLOAD_BASE=
# Root of the temporary import-job directories. A running job keeps a partial
# download; an interrupted job keeps it for 24 hours so it can continue. The
# default is `<system temp>/dbrainx-media-imports`.
# DBRAINX_MEDIA_IMPORT_DIR=
# Encrypts the saved site logins with AES-256-GCM. A deployment must set a stable
# value. When this is empty, the server generates a random per-machine secret and
# persists it at `<repo>/.cache/media-cookie-secret`; the server still starts. A
# generated secret belongs to one machine, so set the value for anything that
# restarts or scales. Print a value with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# DBRAINX_MEDIA_COOKIE_SECRET=
# Login method A (read the cookies of a browser on this machine) is offered when
# the server is a local backend: `DBRAINX_LOCAL_MODE=1` (or `true`), or
# `DBRAINX_DESKTOP=1` (the desktop app sets it), or `NODE_ENV` is not
# `production`. A local development server is therefore local by default. A web
# deployment leaves this empty and runs in production, which hides method A.
# DBRAINX_LOCAL_MODE=

# --- Storage policy --------------------------------------------------------
# Development reads these values from this file: `pnpm dev` starts PocketBase
# with the same environment, and the PocketBase upload hook enforces them, as
# does the API. A container deployment sets them in `deploy/.env.example`
# instead. Sizes are whole bytes; a suffix such as "10GiB" is not accepted. An
# empty or invalid value falls back to the default shown after it, so a typo
# never locks storage.
#
# Total bytes one user may store, across every workspace and counting derived
# artifacts (a PDF cover, an HLS segment). 0 disables the total quota.
# Default: 10737418240 (10 GiB)
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_USER_QUOTA_BYTES=
# Largest single file per media category. The category is decided by the MIME
# type the browser reports, then by the file extension.
# Defaults: image 33554432 (32 MiB), video 2147483648 (2 GiB),
# audio 536870912 (512 MiB), pdf 536870912 (512 MiB),
# webpage 16777216 (16 MiB), other 268435456 (256 MiB).
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_MAX_VIDEO_BYTES=
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_MAX_AUDIO_BYTES=
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_MAX_PDF_BYTES=
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_MAX_WEBPAGE_BYTES=
# DBRAINX_STORAGE_MAX_OTHER_BYTES=
